 "decoded_text": "The function `ccm_finish_reduced` takes a `tag_len` parameter which is used directly in the `memcpy` call on line 10.\n\n1.  **Line 3**: `full_tag` is a fixed-size array of 16 bytes.\n2.  **Line 5**: `ccm_compute_full_tag` fills `full_tag`.\n3.  **Line 10**: `memcpy(tag, full_tag, tag_len)` copies `tag_len` bytes from `full_tag` to `tag`.\n\nThe vulnerability is a **buffer overflow** (or out-of-bounds read/write depending on the context of `tag`, but primarily an out-of-bounds read from `full_tag` if `tag_len > 16`, or a buffer overflow in `tag` if the caller allocated less than `tag_len`).\n\nSpecifically:\n- If `tag_len > 16`, `memcpy` will read out of bounds from `full_tag` (stack buffer overflow/read).\n- If the caller allocated `tag` with a size smaller than `tag_len`, this is a heap/stack buffer overflow.\n\nThe standard for CCM (Counter with CBC-MAC) allows tag lengths from 4 to 16 bytes. The function does not validate `tag_len` against the maximum size of `full_tag` (16) or the minimum size (4). The lack of bounds checking on `tag_len` before the `memcpy` is the primary bug. The bug is introduced at the point where the unchecked `tag_len` is used to perform the memory copy.\n\nLine 10 is where the unsafe operation occurs.\n\nAnswer: 10",
